 "notes_for_editor": "Newspaper searches (Chronicling America, MD papers) found only incidental hits (e.g. Evening Capital 1904-10-19 mentions a transfer to the front light; 1907 hits concern the upper range), not used. The Lighthouse Board reports for 1871-1874 are not in the corpus; construction details come from the NRHP nomination, which cites them. The 1911 and 1926 rescues are second-hand (LighthouseFriends, Chesapeake Chapter); the primary Bulletin entries were not located (1926 Bulletin text did not match). The 1921 report page number is a scan page (printed page uncertain). The 1914 Bulletin rescue by Harry Wills belongs to the Rear light and is not used. NRHP says the lens was a temporary fourth-order in 1873 and permanent fifth-order; USLHS lists original lens as fifth-order 1873. The 1955 keeper quote is as quoted by the NRHP. Pile cut depth 23 ft (NRHP) vs 24 ft (LighthouseFriends); extra appropriation $20,000 (NRHP) vs $25,000 (LighthouseFriends). The 1989 and 1936 items rely on the NRHP only. focal_height_m 11.9 = 39 ft per USLHS/LighthouseFriends; record has 6.7 m (22 ft is height to lantern centre). Roster lines carry the archive's person slugs as printed by roster.py. Mentions of a living owner (Cucé) are limited to the public ownership reported by LighthouseFriends. Fact-check 2026-10-27: NRHP dates the $20,000 appropriation 3 March 1873, before the summer 1873 start of work it describes; story now says so. Dwelling description corrected (the kitchen and sitting room are the present layout; the 1874 plan shows two bedrooms). 1899 lamps dated to the fiscal year. NRHP listing date not printed in the form; story says nominated 2002.",
